{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1785392123,"nonce":"Gu1uHOrrqt7WMY/pHFV/t9AGwZU=","target":"72dfe6f738c68d4ccf5512bc1c8b69c3d93aeed1c4b1470f11fe48b32c6261cf","message":"`LogService::new(store, db)` takes a single `Arc\u003cdyn ObjectStore\u003e` — the **cold** store. With `SCARAB_S3_BUCKET` unset it falls back to `LocalDir(\"./.scarab/objects\")`, which on the Helm chart resolves inside the server Pod's `scratch` **emptyDir**. So logs die on every Pod roll and **nothing says so**: object storage is not merely a prerequisite, it is a silent one.\n\nAnd logs are the one data-plane class that **cannot be re-derived** — re-running a Step yields different logs. Cheapest class to keep, only class where loss is final.\n\n- [ ] Log bytes on the Depot's warm volume, keyed `{run, step, attempt}` + chunk sequence, **not** in the CAS (chunks are unique → no dedup; a hash per chunk buys nothing; the ordered offset index is still required)\n- [ ] Postgres keeps byte offsets only, bodies never (unchanged from ADR-0013)\n- [ ] **Live tail untouched** — the control plane still tails Pod stdout and broadcasts in-process for SSE; only the durable write redirects, and replay reads from the Depot\n- [ ] **The read path is three branches, and absence is authoritative** (ADR-0063 part 5): no chunks in the index → the Step genuinely printed nothing, an empty pane truthfully; chunks + bytes present → serve them; chunks + bytes **absent** → \"logs are no longer available\". The sweeper's eviction record *explains* the third branch and must never be what **triggers** it — a lost or reprovisioned volume was evicted by nobody, so there is no row to find, and a record-first implementation hands the reader the blank pane this slice exists to prevent\n- [ ] **A test in which the bytes are removed with no eviction record at all** — the sweeper never ran — and the reader is still told the logs are gone. This is the branch that regresses silently, because the record-first implementation passes every test where eviction did happen\n- [ ] **The Depot states at boot whether its volume is the one the index describes** (ADR-0063 part 6): a marker written on first init, recorded alongside the index, checked on boot; missing or mismatched → one loud line naming it. Without this an operator's only evidence is a trickle of individually correct per-Run absences, none of which says *this storage is not the storage this database describes*\n- [ ] Un-shipped chunks **pinned** against eviction while a durable sink exists; when the sink cannot keep up, eviction resumes and says so rather than filling the volume\n- [ ] External sinks (object store, Loki, VictoriaLogs) are **additional**, never the system of record\n- [ ] Logs get their own retention class + long TTL in the ADR-0050 sweeper, as Artifacts already have\n- [ ] The warm-size gauge's help text stops implying it measures only the CAS (folds in git-bug 1a9df08)\n\n**Not in scope, and deliberately:** per-Attempt log durability (\"were these logs ever copied to a sink?\"). Rejected in ADR-0063's alternatives — for a class with no recompute path it distinguishes two states with the same available action. The asymmetry with ADR-0064 part 5, which does stamp snapshot durability, is intentional and argued there.\n\n**Deliberately open, and this slice decides it by default — read before landing part 1:** whether logs already in the object store stay readable after the move. Pre-migration Attempts keep valid offsets pointing at bytes in a store the new read path no longer consults, so they become unreachable and part 5 reports them as gone. Options and the reasoning are in ADR-0063's *Open — deliberately not decided here*. **Implementing this slice without a decision selects \"pre-migration logs are dropped\", silently.** That outcome is acceptable; arriving at it by not noticing is not. If you land the slice with that default, say so in the commit — the cheap moment for a migration is before the write path moves.","files":null}]}